Introduction
Autonomous driving or self-driving vehicles are no longer a theory since several manufacturers already sell vehicles capable of self-driving in some degree, e.g. Tesla with the Model S [16]. The level of self-driving capability is internationally defined in the SAE taxonomy for autonomous cars SAE J3016 [15]. This definition includes a range of levels from Level 0 for a vehicle with no self-driving capability up to Level 5 where the vehicle is fully capable of driving from A to B without any presence of a human driver. The term ‘highly automated driving’ is also used to describe driving states comparable to the SAE Level 3 [5]. As result of the long development of Artificial Intelligence for cars, self-driving capability is limited to L3 cars for several years from now. In consequence, the driver’s attention is still necessary.
While being in the transition towards road traffic with a significant portion of self-driving SAE L3 [5] vehicles, a driver of such vehicle needs to be able to regain control of the car at any given time and in a short period of time. Especially in a critical driving situation it is necessary for the driver to be able to react within a timeframe of under 7 seconds [7]. This self-driving situation in which the driver’s participation is mostly passive leads to a monotonous situation without a specific task for the driver. A consequence of this is the use of other devices for several side-activities like texting, reading, playing and communicating. As most
of these side-activities distract the driver to a significant extent, there is a responsibility for the manufacturers to ensure the road safety especially in take-over-request situations until self-driving vehicles become SAE L5 capable.
Several factors need to be addressed while researching this topic. Besides the reaction time between the induced signal for a take-over-situation of the vehicle and the driver’s physical reaction, especially the road perception and the situational awareness are very important for the topic of automated driving [12] and can have an impact on the quality of the driver’s takeover reaction.
With our prototype, we evaluate the concept of reducing the distance the driver’s gaze is displaced from the road scene while maintaining its mental activity through a gaze based interaction with the media content representation on a windshield display. We aim towards a significant reduction in reaction time compared to traditional interaction techniques despite actively distracting the drivers gaze. An ongoing study is intended to evaluate whether the reduction of the reaction time is possible as intended. A following deeper study is planned to confirm the results with more diversity in side-activity applications and a focus on situational awareness.
Related Work
With regard to related work, we focus on the following topics: road safety, windshield based displays, and explicit gaze-based interactions on multimedia content.
