Road safety from the viewpoint of a vehicle’s driver includes their behavior behind the steering wheel and their physical condition. Distraction from the process of driving is the main reason for a reduction in road safety [13]. The physical condition of the driver is a second major reason for a reduced safety factor in case the mental activity is too low, e.g. fatigue or sleepiness [10]. An additional factor related to fatigue is boredom [1]. The monotonous self-driving situation naturally makes the driver to pursue pastimes, leading to sideactivities that distract the attention of the driver from the driving situation. Our approach focuses on distraction and therefore combines the reduction of the reaction times with the side-activities itself.
A windshield-based display is similar to a
conventional head-up display in its basic functionality. Where a head-up display is generally limited in size and position on the edge between dashboard and windshield, a dedicated windshield display can utilize up to the full space of the vehicles windshield [8]. With our prototype, we are able to simulate such a windshield display which are still unavailable on the market. We use the full front display area of the simulation, assuming that windshield displays can use this space in the future. The positioning of multimedia content on a windshield can have disadvantages for the driving and safety when placed in the central area of the driver’s viewport [4]. This has to be addressed when evaluating potential content presentation areas. On the other hand, a display near the driver’s viewport provides benefits towards awareness of the driving situation [11].
Gaze-based Interaction extends the traditional use of eye tracking for post-study gaze analysis of a participant’s gaze behavior by analyzing fixations on predefined content areas (areas of interest) in real-time in order to generate artificial gaze events in the system. This can be used to control system functions by the participant’s gaze alone. In a simulated driving environment, the use of gaze for the explicit control of multimedia functions is technically viable [9]. Compared to this earlier approach for gaze interactions on separate displays aside the simulated driving, we use gaze-based interactions within the driving simulation itself. This enables the research for gaze based interactions closest possible to the center of the driving situation without losing perception of the road.
Prototype Concept
We use a Dikablis 3 marker based head-mounted gaze tracker [14]. The prototype for our research is a combination of a script-based extension of our driving simulator for representation of third-party sideactivities and a separate server tool for the link between our gaze tracker and the simulation. This tool collects the raw gaze data from the eye tracker and calculates area of interest matches based on marker detection. The tool sends its generated events back into the driving simulation for further gaze based media events. With this setup, we can simulate different sideactivity representations of secondary media content, e.g. a Sudoku Game (see figure 1), on a simulated windshield display within the context of the driving simulation.
