In comparison to the prototype implementation, the traditional implementation uses a stand-alone tablet mounted aside the steering wheel for the representation of the same side-activity task. This position resembles a common mounting position in today’s cars whether it is a similar tablet or a dashintegrated touch-panel (see figure 2). At the take-over the simulator sends an audio queue and a visual indicator on the windshield in order to warn the driver. Our prototype presents an additional visual signal on the Sudoku Game as well as fading the game content.
Study Design
The first batch of simulations running on our prototype is an extension to our ongoing long-time study for the evaluation of learning effects for take-over requests of self-driving vehicles. This extension is dedicated to the proposed reduction of reaction times using gaze based interactions with respect to an increased potential for simulator sickness symptoms [3]. We want to verify our hypothesis of a possible reduction of the reaction time before testing a deeper prototype with several implemented media applications and a specific noninvasive task for road perception and situational awareness in road situations that lead to a critical takeover request. This first study is planned to use a total of 60 participants driving over 5 weeks for 45min per drive. While driving, the participants are asked to play a Sudoku game on the windshield display as a consistent and intense distraction task. The interaction with the game is based on their gaze as input mechanism, until the vehicle prompts a take-over request with textual warning on the windshield and an audio queue. Each drive contains at least two standard take-over situations and one additional random critical take-over events. For comparison, a control group will
play Sudoku on a traditional tablet-based side-activity, using touch as input mechanism. After each drive, the participant fills a questionnaire regarding their perceived user experience (Likert Scale) and mental effort (RSME [2]), acceptance of the prototype and level of situational awareness. Based on this study, we plan to conduct a second study dedicated to a more diverse availability of side activities in order to avoid task related effects and with a focus on measurement of the situational awareness for the road scene.
Expectations
The first study in our two-step-approach will be completed at the time of this conference. We expect to see a significant decrease in reaction time of the driver when prompted for a take-over request using our windshield-prototype solution in comparison to the traditional use of a separate device with the same type of side activity used, e.g. tablet.
Discussion
With the presentation of the first findings at the conference, we look forward to discuss our concept and its potential for a future application in actual selfdriving vehicles. For our further research we need to compare to other new approaches that may not be published yet.
Conclusion
This paper describes our study design and prototype implementation. We expect to reduce the driver’s reaction times in take-over-situations by presenting media information on the windshield space around the driving viewport, and by employing the driver’s gaze as input mechanism. First study results will be provided at the conference.
